{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ade109cbd714468f","type":"text","text":"## FCAS - Watt Clarity 6/06\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/06june-regulation-fcas-trend/?_gl=1*ujguto*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODA5NjA0NzUkbzIkZzAkdDE3ODA5NjA0NzUkajYwJGwwJGgw\n\nThere are 10 types of FCAS\n- 2x Regulation (Raise and Lower)\n- 6x Contingency (Raise and Lower)\n- 2x Fast Frequency Response \n\nArticle provides good sources for reading on the Australian FCAS market \n\nThe question for the FCAS market in the future is\n1. The rise of battery storage and the deployment of auto-bidders\n2. The need to ramp up the amount of Regulation FCAS enabled to confront the increasingly visible limitations of the Semi-Scheduled Category\n3. Increasing conflicts between Regulation FCAS Services and the provision of Primary Frequency Regulation (That differs in how they're triggered, response speed, and compensation structure). Note that PFR are inherent in scheduled and semi-scheduled generators and are traditionally unpaided. There are automatic responses to local frequency changes that acts in milliseconds, enabled by inertia. Comparatively, Regulation FCAS is dispatched centrally by AEMO via AGC that occurs every 4 seconds to bring the frequency back tot he 50Hz target. The ancillary service is traded on a spot market.\n\nFCAS prices can have periods of sustained volatility in island and potential islanding events.(where generator continues to supply power even though the grid is isolated from the main network)","x":-466,"y":80,"width":381,"height":320,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ea836e8a406243d3","type":"text","text":"## Semi dispatch cap causes downwards frequency drift - 7/06\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com/market-reform/general-reform-initiatives/semischeduled/sustainable-or-scalable/\n\nSemi-scheduled generators are required to operate in two distinct modes, \n\n- Semi Dispatch cap OFF - Generator can generate to its natural resource limit\n- Dispatch cap ON - due to constraints in the transmission or being dispatched down due to increasing incidence of negative price in the dispatch (priced out). The unit must take account of the Dispatch Target when it operates.\n\nFor a fully scheduled generator, the target is the number the generator must generate, while for semi-scheduled plant, the target is a cap.\n\nSDI- the semi dispatch intervals is a 5 minute forecast. This SDC causes downward deviation in generator availability.\n\nThis will cause a down drift in frequency, so a larger volume of raise regulation FCAS whenever SDC is on.\n- VRE units with SDC on and availabiltiy above cap is being inhibited from delivering PFR because this would take their output above cap and they would become non-conforming.\n- We are wasting the capability of those resources with additional capabilities to resist downward frequency drift","x":40,"y":80,"width":360,"height":100,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"1f20c93413989ad3","type":"text","text":"# Frequency Performance Payments (24/03/25) \n\nImplemented last year, it's a replacement to the causer's pay process for the allocation of Regulation FCAS.\n\nA good summary of the FCAS market in Australia.\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 081239.png]]\n\nEssentially FPP changes such that PFR is able to receive money from the PFR money pot for helping alleviate frequency deviations.\n- There's some indication that this PFR pot of money will be larger than the regulation FCAS pot of money.\n- This is as the FPP provides both an incentive for PFR and penalty for contributing to frequency fluctuations. This reduces the growing requirement for the increasing volume of regulation FCAS.\n\nUnder the causers pay methodology, we saw generators completely avoiding the FCAS cost recovery process, as we see when units obtain a 0 MPF (market Participant Factor) (That means that all generators will pay for causing frequency deviations)\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 082757.png]]\n\nThere is also however, a penalty and incentive scheme built into this mechanism, (0 sum gain), where the penalty occurred will equal to the incentive payments. \n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 083209.png]]\n\n- The primary beneficiary are the batteries and coal, who are the price-setters in the market. This may cause FCAS regulation price inflation.\n- This mechanism might slow the growth in requirements for Regulation FCAS, helping to constrain Market Power and keep prices low.\n\nhttps://www.etsi.energy/\nLook at this link to understand the dynamics down to the individual generator","x":40,"y":231,"width":360,"height":73,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"979899e42e8150e9","type":"text","text":"# Review of Frequency Performance Payments - 44 days in\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2025/07/frequency-performance-payments-44-days-in/\n\nHighlights potential for the FPP recovery costs to be higher than the regulation recovery cost.\n\nThe FPP adds in a default contribution factor to generators, where regulation FCAS that is used are recovered in proportion to unit performance in the interval through a negative contribution factor. While the cost of regulation FCAS that is not used are recovered in proportion each unit's historical performance, a default contribution factor. So even if a generator did well one day, there will still be a default payment, as opposed to the causer's pay mechanism previously implemented\n\nThe first 44 day data shows that the recovery cost is greater than the payments given.","x":520,"y":231,"width":340,"height":145,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ae834cd96fb5c776","type":"text","text":"## Ancillary Services","x":-420,"y":-252,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8a6ac187b079e234","type":"text","text":"# A new view on system strength - Watt Clarity - 4/06\n\nArgues that GFL is not inherently bad, just different, and that a low inertia grid actually has many advantages when compared to a high inertia grid. We should focus instead on better transmission capability and optimising GFL to GFM ratio rather than imposing penalties on solar and wind projects for using GFL inverters. \n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/reframing-the-grid-discussion-from-system-strength-to-system-behaviour/?_gl=1*1uizbah*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODA1MjY3MTIkbzEkZzAkdDE3ODA1MjY3MTIkajYwJGwwJGgw","x":-125,"y":-110,"width":386,"height":100,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"e825a77c1514c066","type":"text","text":"# Transmission Shortfall in India - Ember - 5/06\n\nTransmission backlog caused by supply gap for HVDC, fragmented land ownership, right of way disputes, and biodiversity clearances. This, along with concentrated solar and wind, caused 300GWh of solar curtailment in Q1 alone. \n\nReactionary planning of transmission lines to generation buildout. You can fix this by adding stronger regulatory support for BESS resource pooling that creating a government backed body that can aggregate projects to contract to BESS developers, and allow BESS to be procured as a grid asset to be socialised across transmission charges.","x":71,"y":-376,"width":381,"height":74},
		{"id":"f7cb0231681c42fc","type":"text","text":"# Electricity Market","x":-85,"y":-960,"width":301,"height":102,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"74648b2066572d59","type":"text","text":"## NEM","x":-525,"y":-800,"width":250,"height":50,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8cf11538bf913076","type":"text","text":"## News\n","x":-1072,"y":-671,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"3ed94f554e0243ad","type":"text","text":"# Grid Planning","x":-633,"y":-474,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d602014597b26ba5","type":"text","text":"# Australian Energy usage and the Kardashev scale - Renew Economy- 4/06\n\nArticle talks about Australian renewable curtailment and how it had reached 7,200 GWh in 2025, doubling figure in 2024. Would be interesting to look more into the breakdown of this.\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/the-kardashev-scale-australias-staggering-energy-transition-and-its-untapped-opportunity/","x":-1778,"y":-444,"width":386,"height":169,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"1dec40a9aa655e7f","type":"text","text":"## Tariffs on modified steel projects will impact renewable costs in Australia - RenewEconomy 6/06\n\nRetrospective tariffs of 48% on hollow steel tubing from China, Taiwan, Malaysia, and South Korea. The material is used for making the torque tubes that support the mounting of solar modules.\n\nAustralian manufacturers are arguing that the items are being sold below cost price and interferes with domestic ability to stay competitive.\n\nThis could affect GWs of solar projects starting construction. Exact cost will vary, but the decision can take months to resolve and lead to delays for planned, anticipated, and under construction projects. ","x":-1698,"y":-163,"width":381,"height":100,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5f9e5d0e0c538eec","type":"text","text":"# One year of FPP\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/fpp-anniversary-solarfarms/\n\n","x":91,"y":588,"width":361,"height":52,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"cc3a877b7155caf8","type":"text","text":"# Impacts of FPP on solar farms\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/frequency-penalties-are-costing-some-solar-farms-millions-but-this-is-the-market-working/\n\nSolar penalties are paying for $32$% of all frequency performance payments between June last year and April this year, despite only producing $8.7$% of total generation on the east coast.\n\n- This is mainly due to variability in weather that causes sudden changes outside of their control\n- The big winners have been batteries, followed by coal and hydro.\n\t- ~={orange}Why are they the big winners?=~ \nTotal frequency-related costs average at $0.38/MWh over the initial June-April period, approximately $1.23$% of wholesale market revenue. \n\nHowever, you can be in the same region and still be on both sides of the coin. Wrong forecasts can penalize you heavily, and therefore the operating strategy of the SF can affect FPP cost recovery heavily.\n\nSFs are increasingly operating at night to take advantage of their batteries. But although they aren't generating power, they are still charged as a grid participant for ancillary and frequency control services.\n- Furthermore, they are also charged extra in the middle of the day, during the solar peak when they aren't really making any money.\n","x":690,"y":516,"width":422,"height":144,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"be0b76f82ada8567","type":"text","text":"# Hibernation traps and administrative lag for high velocity resources - Renew Economy 13.05.26\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/05/the-velocity-of-flexibility-avoiding-the-iprr-hibernation-trap/\n\nThere is currently a lag between when VPPs and Voluntary Scheduled Resources (VSPs) are technically available and when they are legally allowed to bid. \n\nThis comes from the status notice obligated by the Integrating Price Responsive Resources (IPRR) framework, where VSR and VPPs have to update their administrative status after long downtimes (around long scheduled maintenance or Christmas)\n\nThese services may be sidelined due to administrative lag when it returns from hibernation.\n- E.g. when your resource comes back on early, you may face penalties for a deviation that your software can handle, but administrative status does not permit\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-22 145241.png]]\n\nIn the case of a hibernation (downtime > 14 days), VIM incentive payments are paused and a formal status change must be lodged with AEMO.\n\nThese lags makes resources unable to respond to price spike events and artificially inflates the WACC for VPP projects, as investor sees that regulatory friction can sideline their asset, they will demand higher returns to compensate for that risk.\n\nTo fix this, the article author suggest the use of a telemetry validated reserve to replace manual notice periods.\n- Also implement compliance buffers to protect providers and encourage them to return to market sooner.\n- Have dynamic VIM re-activation such that the VIM incentive eligibility is restored the moment the plant becomes active.","x":-840,"y":-143,"width":424,"height":140,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d83605c145bc7c71","type":"text","text":"# Operational Forecasts - AEMO / WattClarity\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/05/an-introduction-to-operational-forecasting-at-aemo/\n\nThe Operational Forecast Team is responsible for providing operational forecast capabilities. \n\nThe produce the Forecast Uncertainty Measure (FUM) which is used to determine the lack of reserve triggers.\n\n- They make models and tools to help predict constraints and dispatch in the neat term.\n- Also support connect asset by evaluating Energy Conversion Models in the NER3.7B, check SCADA signal consistency. They also onboard non scheduled registration applications to see if forecasting is required. (Participants can also submit their own forecasts and the OPF assist with this)\n- They also keep an eye on the weather conditions for potential disruptions to VRE","x":-1418,"y":-3,"width":346,"height":83,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"414f6481b022479f","type":"text","text":"# Addressing stochastic variability in forecasting\n\nGrid forecasting in the NEM and ESOO is done on stochastic data but plans for a deterministic outcome, which is the central scenario. However this does not address the worst case scenario, which is what we should be planning for. \n\n- The article calls for a reframing of how we can better plan for a grid that is becoming more variable and weather dependent.\n- When you plan for the centre, you strip the headroom required for the worst.\n\nThe article calls for us to move beyond POE10 and POE50, drawing on much larger weather datasets.\n- Bring gas demand and transport limits into analysis, too often are they left at the edge of the model when they are the system's reserves\n- Add wargaming, stress testing, and red team/blue team exercise into the grid\n\n\n","x":-1001,"y":-279,"width":359,"height":87,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d699e9c47f97fa2b","x":-840,"y":-990,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"# Grid Operations"},
		{"id":"924f6a21f79e3c7b","x":-1180,"y":-1360,"width":343,"height":280,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"# Performance report for events above $5000/MWh\n\nConsiders the market trends, event or behavior surrounding competition and efficient market operation, where the wholesale market price >$5000/MWh for the 30 minute period.\n- These events drove uo the volume-weighted average price by $7/MWh\n\nThere were 3 separate events in Q4 2025, compared to 7 the previous quarter and 23 the same period last year.\n\n- In all three instance, high prices were driven by low solar and high demand due to high temperatures during the summer\n- One event also contributed to rebidding behavior, where participants withdrew their low price and moved capacity from low to high prices for technical or commercial reason\n\nThe reduction of grid scale solar must be replaced by higher priced generation and reduction of rooftop solar also means that demand needs to be met from the grid.\n\n## Units being unable to ramp up \n\nAround 1700 MW of low priced capacity was unable to be dispatched as units were not able to ramp up quick enough to respond to the pricing event.\n\n- The ramp rates of 9 units across 4 market participants had their ramp rates at a minimum setting. The AEMC has submitted a rule change where generators have to submit a ramp rate that reflect the technical/maximum capacity of the plant, rather than using it to achieve commercial objectives.\n\nAnother constraint was caused by planned network outage in interconnectors that prevented cheap bids from entering the market.\n"},
		{"id":"b5b2f677ee18d637","x":1053,"y":-1013,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"# Thermodynamics\n"},
		{"id":"8b5748dab6df1aa1","type":"text","text":"## Exergy - Online course\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b94HAcQXQY4&list=PLsegmWko7aP_U8oGH5IzRXepXjx5_06k6&index=2\n\nThe work potential of energy, or available energy.\n- When a system is in thermodynamic equilibrium with the environment, then none of that energy system can be used to produce work. This is known as the dead state.\n\t- For example, there is no pressure or temperature gradient\n\nThe total energy can be split into \n- Energy with zero exergy content \n- Exergy of the system\n\nTherefore exergy is the energy that takes you above the equilibrium. And a system delivers the maximum possible work as it undergoes a reversible process from the specified initial state to the dead state, the state of the environment.\n\nKinetic energy and potential energy are $100$% exergy.\n\n## Reversibility and Irreversibility\n\nThe more irreversibility, the less efficient the system. \n- Remember that more entropy = more irreversibility because that's energy which can be work\n\n$W_{rev}$ is the maximum amount of work that can be produced.\n\nWhile the Surroundings work is the work done by or against the surroundings during a process. For example, when pushing down on a cylinder in a constant process,$$W_{surr}=P_0(V_2-V_1)$$\n$W_{useful}=W-W_{surr}$\n\nWhere useful work is the same as work is in steady flow devices, rigid tanks, and cyclic devices, as no work is being done to the surrounding to expand them.\n\nUsing this definition, irreversibility is the difference of reversible and useful work. $$I=W_{rev,out}-W_{useful,out}$$or it can also be defined for the input process $$I=W_{in}-W_{rev,out}$$\nTherefore, irreversibility can be viewed as the lost potential to do work\n\n\n","x":975,"y":-858,"width":406,"height":78,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"deec3acd2e0d58c1","x":901,"y":-699,"width":659,"height":499,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## Calculating the irreversibility of a heat engine\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-24 095707.png]]\n\nRemember that irreversibility is the maximum amount of reversible work that is translated into useful work. In this case, the maximum amount of reversible work is the calculated using the Carnot efficiency. Note that this defines the exergy of the system, we cannot get anymore work out of the energy we put in because we cannot go below the ambient temperature.\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nW_{rev,out} &=\\eta_{th,rev}\\dot{Q_{in}} \\\\\n&=(1-\\frac{T_{sink}}{T_{source}})\\dot{Q_{in}} \\\\\n&= 0.75\\times 500kW \\\\\n\\therefore I &= 375-180=195kW \\text{ is the irreversibility}\n\\end{align}$$\n\n## Second law efficiency\nThis allows us to find the second ;aw efficiency, we describes the percentage of maximum reversible work we got out of the system\n\n$$\\eta_{II}=\\frac{180}{375}\\times 100\\%=48\\%$$\n\n"}
	],
